\par
A tragic flaw or error \par
\par
For Aristotle, the value of tragedy lies in its healing effects on those in the audience who suffer from more extreme negative emotional states. He states that, by witnessing tragedy, feelings of pity and fear are generated, leading to the audience being purified of these negative emotions. Herein lies the value of tragedy, a view that is questioned by the more critical opinions of this dramatic form as espoused by Hans-Thies Lehmann. The curative process of katharsis influences those in the audience who are susceptible to damaging emotion through a sort of second-hand discharge by witnessing it being acted out on stage \f1\emdash  it offers \lquote the masses medicine for insight\rquote  (Lehmann, p. 20). This understanding can only be attained by the audience through an awareness of the central character\rquote s hamartia, which is Aristotle\rquote s term meaning an \lquote error\rquote . \par
\par
When attempting to apply this idea of hamartia to Early Modern drama, conventionally it has often been seen as a moral error emerging out of a character\rquote s internal flaw, such as the Duchess\rquote s lust that leads to her destruction. Superficially, Webster\rquote s play seems to comply with this idea, perhaps suggesting the influence of Aristotle on the text\rquote s composition. When the pregnant Duchess eats the apricots given to her by Bosola, he remarks on her \lquote most vulturous\rquote  gluttony (\lquote How greedily she eats them!\rquote ), an act that may symbolically point the audience towards her lust in taking Antonio as a sexual partner. \par
\par
However, it is the villains of the play who link women\rquote s actions to lust, who are depicted as knowingly imposing a \lquote lusty widow\rquote  stereotype on the Duchess: \lquote women like that part which, like the lamprey, hath ne\rquote er a bone in\rquote t\rquote . The Aragonian brothers are unlikely cultural commentators on goodness, with this sexual innuendo hardly serving to highlight a moral flaw. If anything, Webster downplays the Duchess\rquote s lustful nature. According to Christina Luckyj, Webster\rquote s main source for the play\rquote s storyline (William Painter\rquote s The Palace of Pleasure) depicts the Duchess as far more sexually motivated in comparison. For Painter, the Duchess is someone who \lquote steppeth out of the right tracte\rquote  through having too much sexual desire, whereas in Webster her actions are depicted as linked to more prosaic personal needs. \par
\par
Overall, these ideas cast doubt on the notion that The Duchess of Malfi represents a criticism of female sinfulness. Parallel to this point, Sean McEvoy\rquote s argument is that for more didactic textual readings the play \lquote fails to provide some of the moral or religious uplift which\'85critics felt a tragedy should have\rquote  (McEvoy, p. 133). A woman desiring her own choice of sexual partner cannot be designated a moral flaw, problematising a simplistic application of Aristotle\rquote s ideas on tragedy to Webster\rquote s play. \par
\par
An error of judgement \par
\par
More accurately, a character\rquote s hamartia is more of a \lquote mistake\rquote  that is a dramatic turning point in their fortunes and is therefore more a matter of action than innate morality. For Aristotle, tragedy is characterised by this kind of \lquote error\rquote  rather than a moral shortcoming. These errors can be made by characters being fully unaware of a situation or through unintended moral consequences. However, in both cases, they should not be as a result of wilful mischief. So while the Duchess comments on her secret marriage, \lquote I have not gone about in this to create any new world or custom\rquote , indicating that her needs are conventional and not politically radical in nature, her actions still impact on those loved ones close to her, what Aristotle calls her philoi. \par
\par
On the other hand, the Duchess is also depicted as normalising this marital decision, a situation suggested by the stereotypical heterosexual domestic arrangements that assert themselves at night-time despite Antonio and the Duchess\rquote s class differences (Antonio: \lquote my rule is only in the night\rquote ). Webster seems reluctant to depict the Duchess\rquote s actions as a mistake, supported by his use of sententiae (rhyming couplets, often placed at the end of a scene in order to reinforce a moral point). However, at the end of Act 1 Scene 1 Cariola functions as a kind of commentator on the Duchess\rquote s actions, speaking without the use of a sententia and in a more undecided manner, on what motivates her mistress, the \lquote spirit of greatness or of woman\rquote  \emdash  that is, does she act heroically or in a rash manner in seducing Antonio? This ambiguity, lacking the moral certainty of a sententia, suspends judgement on the Duchess\rquote s \lquote error\rquote , shifting the play away from more traditional Aristotelian readings of the play. \par
\par
This point can be further emphasised by contrasting the play\rquote s treatment of Bosola and Antonio. As a conventionally Christian moral character, Antonio seeks compassion from the Cardinal through a personal visit, with the hope that it will \lquote draw the poison out of him\rquote , meaning that it may lead to a reconcilement between them. Delio is shown assisting with this move by bringing one of Antonio\rquote s sons to support him: \par
\par
I\rquote ll fetch your eldest son and second you: \par
It may be that the sight of his own blood \par
Spread in so sweet a figure, may beget \par
The more compassion. \par
\par
(Act 5 Scene 5) \par
\par
The end result of this reasonable act of seeking absolution makes Antonio a victim, as he is accidentally slain by Bosola in the dark. Indeed, the audience is encouraged to laugh at Antonio, seen in his darkly humorous dying remarks on his fate. In his ironic reply to the servant\rquote s question \lquote Where are you sir?\rquote , Antonio responds with \lquote very near my home\rquote , commenting on his proximity to death and therefore heaven, rather than his unknown location in the darkness as the servant expects. By comparison, a malcontent Bosola displays more extreme and less rational emotion, seen in his resentment (Antonio: \lquote he rails at those things which he wants\rquote ), a tendency to tears and misery (Bosola says \lquote This is manly sorrow\rquote  and \lquote Break heart\rquote  on the Duchess\rquote s and Antonio\rquote s deaths), and psychopathic traits through his murder of the servant who may raise the alarm (Bosola: \lquote There\rquote s for you first\rquote ). \par
\par
Emotions and morals \par
\par
Both Antonio and Bosola are tragic characters, as their hamartia stems from one\rquote s decision to marry above himself and the other\rquote s decision to be employed by the Aragonian brothers. Yet, from an Aristotelian point of view, are these truly errors of action that lead to their destruction? Antonio fails to prosper despite being a Christian and man of integrity, suggesting his death is more a matter of moral naivety than choice of marriage partner, whereas Bosola ultimately rejects corruption in favour of a kind of moral avenger status (\lquote The weakest arm is strong enough that strikes with the sword of justice\rquote ). Webster does not mock Bosola at the end of the text, and if anything the audience is encouraged to admire him for his actions in destroying corruption, even though he is destroyed himself in the attempt. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The final sententia of the play (\lquote Integrity of life is fame\rquote s best friend, / Which nobly, beyond death, shall crown the end\rquote ) could apply as equally to Bosola as the Duchess. It is also interesting that in the play Bosola has more lines than the Duchess. Both are important characters, as they act out of emotion, sometimes even recklessly, and this is depicted as something to be respected. For Webster, emotional excess seems to be often valorised above rational behaviour, a major difference to Aristotle\rquote s conception of tragedy. In fact, for Webster tragedy seems to have more to do with transgression, or more simply, stepping over boundaries, leading Roberta Barker to call the Duchess a \lquote regal over-reacher\rquote  (Callaghan, p. 75). \par
\par
Tragedy as transgression \par
\par
This transgressive view leads us to consider how the tragic elements of the play may be more closely linked to how it explores challenges to social norms in the Early Modern period. Instead of being a kind of \lquote fearful madness\rquote  and therefore a simple error of action, the Duchess\rquote s secret marriage may have something heroic about it as a \lquote dangerous venture\rquote . Lehmann has called this kind of transgressive phenomenon \lquote overstepping\rquote , where the tragic involves crossing the boundaries of what is considered acceptable or normal to society as a means of testing or even attacking those values. In the case of The Duchess of Malfi, this involves confronting what are considered the accepted conditions under which marriage can take place in the Early Modern era. \par
\par
Leah S. Marcus has speculated that Early Modern audiences might have seen the Duchess\rquote s clandestine marriage as a heroic rather than immoral act (Webster 2009). While the setting of the play is in Italy, audiences would have regarded the play\rquote s events as relevant to historical developments in England at that time. Marcus\rquote s argument is that, in 1597 and 1604, new canons were issued by the English Church that attempted to restrict clandestine marriage through changes to the law. For example, legal marriage now required the announcement of the banns over three Sundays or a marriage licence from a bishop. In addition, marriages had to take place in a parish church with a licensed cleric officiating over the ceremony. The failure to comply with these new restrictions could result in punishment from an ecclesiastical court, such as through a fine or an embarrassing public penance. Marcus also points out that the new canon laws on marriage were particularly unpopular with Puritans: \par
\par
Opposition to the new canon law was particularly prevalent in strongly Puritan parishes, where there was resistance to the use of rings and the ceremonialism of Anglican marriage ritual. \par
\par
(Webster 2009, p. 36) \par
\par
Despite the changes to canon law, clandestine marriage continued for a variety of reasons. First, it was popular with couples of unequal class status. In The Duchess of Malfi, the class differences between Antonio and the Duchess are made clear in the seduction scene in Act 1 Scene 1. Second, some widows who wanted to protect their wealth from a first marriage married secretly when they remarried. This is particularly pertinent to Webster\rquote s play, as Ferdinand reveals he objects to a second marriage as he has designs on the Duchess\rquote s money (\lquote I had a hope \emdash  had she continued widow \emdash  to have gained an infinite mass of treasure by her death\rquote ). \par
\par
Finally, there were those who refused to acknowledge the authority of ecclesiastical courts, a contempt perhaps hinted at in the Duchess\rquote s remark on clandestine marriage: \lquote\rquote tis the Church that must but echo this\rquote . Overall, rather than an erroneous act, Marcus views the Duchess\rquote s clandestine marriage as \lquote an act of heroic resistance\rquote  (2011, p. 114), with which Early Modern audiences would have been sympathetic because of the imposition of marriage law that limited their personal freedoms. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Clandestine marriage and social resistance \par
\par
Following the view that clandestine marriage is a form of social resistance, it becomes difficult to view The Duchess of Malfi as a play about the tragic consequences of lust as a fatal flaw in the Aristotelian sense. Here, tragedy seems to be more about \lquote overstepping\rquote , a transgressive and critical exploration of the consequences of sexual regulation in Early Modern England disguised in a continental setting. The fault lies more with society than with individuals, with the Aragonian brothers perhaps symbolically representing the social bodies that place limits on self-determination. In this context, clandestine marriage in Webster\rquote s play can be seen as a comment on this restrictive situation that no doubt struck a chord with audiences of Webster\rquote s time. Interestingly, by the 1640s ecclesiastical courts were so unpopular with the public that they were abolished. \par
\par
Today, the continued popularity of The Duchess of Malfi is possibly due to its themes of sexual regulation, which continue to be relevant as judgements on the relationship choices of others continue to be made from some quarters of society. \par
